WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.041 --> 00:00:14.642
was no way out of that cabin.

2
00:00:15.902 --> 00:00:22.206
The window wasn't big enough for even a dog to go through, the chimney was too narrow, and

3
00:00:22.246 --> 00:00:23.726
the door was solid oak.

4
00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:29.169
But on one wall of the cabin a blanket was nailed up to keep out the wind.

5
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:35.309
I searched the cabin for a useful tool and finally found one lying on a roof beam.

6
00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:38.131
It was an old rusty saw.

7
00:00:39.513 --> 00:00:43.976
While Pap was away, I began to saw at a log in the wall that was hidden by

8
00:00:44.016 --> 00:00:44.517
the blanket.

9
00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:49.881
I was almost finished sawing through the log when Pap came back again.

10
00:00:51.062 --> 00:00:54.285
Pap made me go to the boat and get the things he had bought.

11
00:00:55.686 --> 00:01:02.012
There was a twenty-kilogram sack of cornmeal, a big piece of bacon, and a fifteen-liter jug of whiskey.

12
00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:07.479
I carried the supplies to the cabin, and then I sat down on the bow of the boat

13
00:01:07.519 --> 00:01:07.959
to rest.

14
00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:14.424
I decided that when Pap got drunk and fell asleep I would finish sawing a hole in the

15
00:01:14.444 --> 00:01:15.664
cabin and run away.

16
00:01:16.765 --> 00:01:20.027
I'd take Pap's gun and I would live by hunting and fishing.

17
00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:26.332
I'd go so far away that neither Pap nor the Widow Douglas would ever find me.

18
00:01:27.532 --> 00:01:29.033
But luck didn't go my way.

19
00:01:30.214 --> 00:01:34.609
Instead of falling asleep after drinking his whiskey, Pap had terrible visions.

20
00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:39.213
He thought that snakes were crawling all over him and biting him.

21
00:01:40.694 --> 00:01:45.537
His eyes were wild and he grabbed at the air with his hands, knocking things over.

22
00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:50.181
Later in the night, he thought I was the angel of death.

23
00:01:52.062 --> 00:01:58.227
He chased me around the cabin with a knife until he collapsed, but in the morning, he couldn't

24
00:01:58.247 --> 00:01:59.568
remember anything he'd done.

25
00:02:01.004 --> 00:02:05.045
Pap told me to go down to the river and see if we had caught any fish on

26
00:02:05.105 --> 00:02:05.565
our lines.

27
00:02:07.366 --> 00:02:12.087
I saw that the water was rising, and many logs and branches were floating down the river.

28
00:02:13.527 --> 00:02:16.728
And then I saw a big canoe riding the waves like a duck.

29
00:02:17.969 --> 00:02:18.769
No one was in it.

30
00:02:19.909 --> 00:02:24.990
I waded right into the water to get it, and then I paddled it back to shore and

31
00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.511
hid it under some willow branches.

32
00:02:28.891 --> 00:02:33.649
By the time Pap came down to the river, I was taking five catfish off the fishing lines.

33
00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.191
We had breakfast, and then we took a rest.

34
00:02:38.171 --> 00:02:39.912
I was still thinking about my escape.

35
00:02:40.992 --> 00:02:45.014
I needed to make sure that Pap and the Widow Douglas wouldn't try to bring me back.

36
00:02:46.034 --> 00:02:47.315
And then I thought of a way.

37
00:02:48.856 --> 00:02:54.658
Later that day, Pap and I saw nine logs joined together— part of a raft—coming down the river.

38
00:02:55.879 --> 00:02:58.620
We went out in our rowboat and towed them ashore.

39
00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:04.323
Then Pap locked me in the cabin and went off in the rowboat, towing the logs to sell

40
00:03:04.343 --> 00:03:08.006
them. I didn't think he'd be back until the next morning.

41
00:03:10.047 --> 00:03:15.150
I finished sawing through my hidden log until I could push out a piece and escape through the

42
00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:23.216
hole. First I took the big sack of cornmeal down to the canoe, pushed the willow branches aside,

43
00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.097
and put the sack in the canoe.

44
00:03:25.937 --> 00:03:32.350
Then I went back and forth, bringing other things from the cabin: the bacon, the jug of whiskey,

45
00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:33.751
and the coffee and sugar.

46
00:03:35.032 --> 00:03:39.216
I also took a tin cup, two blankets, matches, and fishing lines.

47
00:03:40.557 --> 00:03:41.698
I cleaned out that cabin.

48
00:03:42.799 --> 00:03:44.581
Last of all I took the gun.

49
00:03:45.782 --> 00:03:49.145
The next thing I did was to go hunting and shoot a wild pig.

50
00:03:50.586 --> 00:03:52.167
I brought its body back to the cabin.

51
00:03:53.228 --> 00:03:56.772
Then I took the ax from our woodpile and smashed in the cabin door.

52
00:03:58.428 --> 00:04:02.350
I smeared some of the pig's blood on the floor of the cabin and on the ax.

53
00:04:03.611 --> 00:04:07.553
I pulled a few hairs out of my head and left them sticking to the bloody ax.

54
00:04:09.054 --> 00:04:13.376
I got rid of the pig by carrying it down to the river and throwing it into the

55
00:04:13.436 --> 00:04:17.298
water. Next I loaded a sack with stones.

56
00:04:18.719 --> 00:04:23.662
Starting at the cabin, I dragged the sack through the dirt so that it left a trail all

57
00:04:23.682 --> 00:04:24.762
the way down to the river.

58
00:04:25.863 --> 00:04:28.284
I dumped the sack into the water and it sank.

59
00:04:29.665 --> 00:04:31.546
Then I thought of one more thing I could do.

60
00:04:32.607 --> 00:04:37.050
I took the sack of cornmeal from the canoe up to the cabin, where I cut a small

61
00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:38.050
hole in the bottom.

62
00:04:39.171 --> 00:04:45.094
I dragged it to a nearby lake, and as I walked, I left a trail of cornmeal coming

63
00:04:45.254 --> 00:04:46.455
out of the hole in the bag.

64
00:04:48.516 --> 00:04:52.779
When I got to the lake, I tied up the bottom of the sack with string, so the

65
00:04:52.839 --> 00:04:54.440
cornmeal wouldn't leak anymore.

66
00:04:55.776 --> 00:04:58.639
Then I carried the bag back to the canoe and climbed in.

67
00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:03.923
I paddled a little way down the river to where some willows hung over the bank.

68
00:05:07.846 --> 00:05:09.268
By now it was getting dark.

69
00:05:10.449 --> 00:05:13.451
I ate some food and rested and thought about my plan.

70
00:05:14.712 --> 00:05:18.916
I hoped I had made it look as if robbers had come to the cabin, broken down the

71
00:05:18.956 --> 00:05:23.380
door, killed me with an ax, and then dragged my body down to the water.

72
00:05:24.832 --> 00:05:28.114
People might look for my body in the river, but they'd soon give up.

73
00:05:29.354 --> 00:05:33.396
If they decided to chase the robbers, they'd follow the trail of grain to the lake.

74
00:05:34.536 --> 00:05:36.057
Meanwhile I would get away.

75
00:05:37.538 --> 00:05:41.780
No one would follow me; I could go anywhere on the Mississippi River I wanted.

76
00:05:43.541 --> 00:05:47.262
I decided I would go to Jackson's Island, out in the middle of the river.

77
00:05:48.603 --> 00:05:50.784
I knew it well, and nobody ever went there.

78
00:05:52.188 --> 00:05:54.652
At night I would be able to paddle over to St.

79
00:05:54.712 --> 00:05:57.616
Petersburg and creep around to get the things I needed.
